<section class="example-section">
  <h2>What if I forget part of the setup?</h2>
  <p>This is why the process is documented and the document is shared with heirs in advance. Even if you suffer health problems and can’t remember anything about it your heir will already know what to do. Also the dead man switch will help you and your heir get a reminder about the guide in case all of you forgot, but this is just extra protection.</p>
  <p>Reading the document will reveal what to do.</p>
</section>

<section class="example-section">
  <h2>What if the password manager fails?</h2>
  <p>You should have a backup password manager even if it is just for bitcoin inheritance, consider also adding your email password to ensure they can recover your other credentials, also make sure to include the 2FA password. Two candidates are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://bitwarden.com/help/emergency-access/">Bitwarden requires a paid subscription, but not for the other party</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://proton.me/support/emergency-access">Proton requires a paid subscription, but not for the other party</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Remember that you can export backups of your vault so you can be protected in case one of them goes out of business you will have time to migrate to a new one. This will also work in case of both companies going out of business. However, the chances of both businesses failing while at the same time you are no longer around is extremely rare specially since it will all need to happen in a very short time window of a couple months. So this is not really a concern.</p>
  <p>Document this for your heir so that they can request access.</p>
</section>

<section class="example-section">
  <h2>User provided entropy vs device entropy</h2>
  <p>I am an advocate of removing single points of failure and creating wallets using entropy from different strategies protects precisely against a single faulty mechanism or implementation. This problem is really not a concern for Bitcoin Core, it has been battle tested and the specific feature to get entropy is not updated regularly if at all. However, it is possible that you might run into an obscure scenario where a very specific OS version and hardware combination could result in some uknown case of low entropy. To avoid this extremely rare situation you can use different laptops with different hardware on them and install different OS versions and generate a key per device.</p>
  <p>This problem is more common with wallet hardwares where new ones pop up every year and their implementations have not been battle tested and there is essentially no code review outside the internal team. So the one thing you don't want is to mix good entropy from Bitcoin Core with potentially bad entropy from other implementations. That would not make it more secure.</p>
  <p>If you still feel strongly about introducing custom entropy it is possible, but you need to take extra steps to create a wallet without private keys and import your own private descriptor with your own entropy.</p>
</section>

<section class="example-section">
  <h2>Why run USBGuard?</h2>
  <p>The laptop for the bitcoin dedicated device is very secure in the sense that the attack surface is minimum. The file system is encrypted with a strong passphrase, it is only running verified software and not much outside of the already existing software, you are running it in an isolated network via ethernet, and the usage is limited to bitcoin related operations with bitcoin core and python scripts without external libraries. This means no devices can reach it and so there is no way to deliver a payload to exploit the system other than through the limited attack surface inherit to the bitcion related operations we run. But then there is USB. All it takes is someone including yourself unknowingly connecting a malicious USB device and the whole system becomes compromised. With USBGuard all USB devices that have not been previously authorized are blocked by default so any attempt of introducing malware via BadUSB is impossible.</p>
  <p>It essentially protects against evil maid attacks, but you should still keep those devices properly isolated from people.</p>
</section>
